A few days ago in the alpha-osf-managers mailing list the problem of
having PAGER set and using man was solved:  one must have PAGER as
"more/page -s" or "less -r" to strip off the extraneous garbage.

In my opinion is that this is not a solution: it is a kludge to cover
up a fea^H^H^Hbug.  Why?  Because the extra options are not needed
anywhere else in the about dozen other UNIX environments I use: from
pure BSD, HPUX to AIX, IRIX.  OSF/1's "man" and/or *roff-stuff have
something different from all the others?

(No doubt somebody from DEC will unearth few standards tomes like XPGn or
 POSIXabc and tell me that this behaviour is dictated by
 21.9.31.18-47, 5.B.112-135, 43.5.11, ... :-)

[End Prologue]

My current headache:  how to get Digital-supplied GNU Emacs 18.58's
"man" work correctly.  This leaves the same VT100 garbage around;
the "Cleaning up manual entry for foo..." code in Emacs does not perform
quite the same way as in other UNIX systems.  The VT100 bold on-off
codes are left in the buffer.  What to do?  Nag to DEC?  (done that
already, maybe must raise my voice)  Nag to FSF?  Hack the
DEC-supplied Emacs C/elisp code? (bad, site-dependent)


