> A few days ago in the alpha-osf-managers mailing list the problem of
> having PAGER set and using man was solved:  one must have PAGER as
> "more/page -s" or "less -r" to strip off the extraneous garbage.

Since nobody has yet ventured back that far in the chain, I would say
that the problem is in setting up the man entries to default to VT100
as an output device in the first place.  We will change the man "nroff"
device to "-Tlpr" instead, which is the way all of our other systems
have been set up.  This gives a lowest common denominator output which can
be displayed on almost any terminal, piped into "lpr", etc.  Yet there is
still enough information there in the file for clever programs like "less"
to use screen sequences *appropriate to the current display terminal* such
as BOLD in place of the simple x-BS-x sequences in the file.

Why in this world of open-ness, in the brave new era of OSF, is a system
like "man" set up hard-coded to a specific terminal device?  One look at
the section of the "man" man page that explains how one might print out a
man entry should convince most people that something is wrong...

--
