There seem to be several others shipping OSF/1 kernel technology,
if you care about that sort of thing.  IBM, Hatachi, Intel, etc.
DEC is the only one who is shipping it as the 'on every workstation
on your desk' OS.

See your (and my) next point.

>	If the kernel technology is no longer the focus, and the
>"environment" is, and every vendor's OSF environment is different,
>what's the point of OSF anymore? Is there?

This is where OSF will win big.  IBM commands and libraries are
basically OSF/1 commands and libraries.  They will be even closer
in the next AIX release. HP is talking about 
taking OSF/1 user space in 10.x.  The COSE initiative is using
OSF/1 and SVR4 as its base.

Speaking as a user space guy (and certainly *not* for OSF) the only
think *I* care about is that I have the same command set, which
works in basically the same way.  The same header files, the same
libc routines, etc.  Its taken a while, but everyone seems to be
comming around to what OSF/1 (particularly Release 1.2) has to offer:
	POSIX, XPG4, Internationlization, quality.

As far as "The libc OSF ships", there is nothing in our libc RS6000
specific.  In fact, as Simon P. has pointed out in other posts, OSF
