>If OSF is going to have the "one OS" model of things, then OSF/1 AD
>needs to be either renamed or made the same as OSF/1.

	Lots of these questions are at the library level and so forth.
My query was really related to a higher level function of the system.
Will versions of OSF use (for example) "setld" or some similar tool?
Will they all use the same version of cron and come with the same
version of /etc/termcap? Will they all use the same version of curses?
Will they all ship the same version of X, with the same level of
support for PEX, and the same set of tools?

	When someone tells me "one operating system to rule them all,
one operating system to bind them..." I'm actually less interested
in the kernel than I am in the system's "interface" to the user. It's
that, more than anything else, that has made UNIX a notable un-success
in the operating systems area - all the gratuitious vendor differences.

	In short, is there "value added" with OSF or is it really going
to be the same operating system across all those platforms? If not, then
OSF's a moot point - great, you produced a nifty look and feel for a
window manager, and some good vaporware that may get lots of use someday,
if anyone ever actually starts shipping something more stable than
"advanced development kits".

	"Value added" is a disease, a blight, a pox. The little festering
differences between various vendor UNIXes make a mockery of "open systems"
and achieve nothing other than making Windows NT look good.

mjr.


