In article <1993Apr14.124628.20840@porthos.cc.bellcore.com> wws@iscp.bellcore.com (Wayne Scott) writes:

   keeping track of the PIDs so I can terminate them when the application is shut
   down.  I can NOT terminate the xterm PIDs with kill(pid, SIGTERM); but I CAN
   terminate them with kill(pid, SIGKILL).  However, when an rlogin is done from
   the xterm, the rlogin is still active AFTER the xterm has been killed.
   When I logout of the xterm windows with <ctrl-d>, the xterm AND rlogin go away.

If you have xterm sources, you can easily see that there is something
'magically' going on for System V. I had the same problem with a 'tool server'
that creates new processes on remote requests. Worked fine on Sun, but on
a SGI one could not kill the xterms unless with brutal force, especially not
with INTR, HUP and QUIT. My solution was to create a new process group for
the forked process, like this:

            OS_signal(SIGHUP, (OS_SigType)SIG_DFL);
            OS_signal(SIGINT, (OS_SigType)SIG_DFL);
            OS_signal(SIGQUIT, (OS_SigType)SIG_DFL);
            OS_signal(SIGHUP, (OS_SigType)SIG_DFL);
            
            // ensure that the exec'ed process has its own group,
            // otherwise some programms in SysVR3 will ignore
            // the terminal-signals (SIGHUP, SIGINTR, SIGQUIT, SIGTERM)
            // like xterms on SGI IRIX..... (mj)
            
            setsid(); 
            
            execvp( path, &argv[0] );

This is not an OSF feature, but rather common to System V and POSIX - or
xterm's handling of signals in case of beeing compiled for SysV. The code
checks if the pid == pgrp and only then reacts on the signals (as I recall..) 

Hope that helps,   Michael
 
