> performance?  Any and all information you could give me about actually
> running an Alpha would be appreciated.

I've ported a truckload of freeware to Alpha (as have others) for the Alpha
OSF/1 freeware CDROM, and most of it was a piece of cake.  Sure does show
off sloppy practices, though; at this point I feel I can nearly tell the
age and experience of a program's author by looking at the output from a
"make".  If casts are wrong, pointers are sloppy, data structures are
poorly designed ... then the author is an undergraduate under 21 years
old.  (Mentally?)  Nonetheless, even lots of sloppy code turns out to work.
Take a look through the freeware CD -- you'll see what I mean.

Really makes you appreciate good design and good coding practices.  You'll
find examples of those on the CD also.

I've also sysadmined an Alpha OSF/1 system, and by God, it's UNIX!  You'll
find it very familiar, but you will want to RTFM as well.

But what I really mean to say here is that my experience, our experience
internally in general, and what I hear from customers -- real customers who've
actually done substantial work with Alpha -- is that generally things work,
they work well, and moving things to Alpha OSF/1 is not to be feared.  Actually
most of the customers who've been working on Alpha OSF/1 for a while are
delighted with it, something you can't tell just by reading these newsgroups,
which aren't representative of the real paying customers of the real world,
and certainly not of the software vendors and OEMs.  My recommendation: buy
one. :-)

