There were several articles about OSF/1 AD, and I'd like to correct
a few things mentioned in them.

First, OSF/1 AD is a Mach 3 server that runs on top a Mach 3 kernel.
Please don't call it a kernel!

From: jet@boxer.nas.nasa.gov (J. Eric Townsend)
> As I understand it, software
> development follows a complicated loop that goes something like
> OSF->Intel->other compan(y|ies)->OSF, yet it's still OSF/1AD.

And the "OSF" in this picture consists of three different groups
of engineers: Engineering (responsible for existing Mach 2.5 kernel),
and the Research Institute staffs at Grenoble (responsible for the
OSF/1 Mach 3 server) and Cambridge (responsible for AD).  While
OSF engineering was developing OSF/1 1.1 (and 1.2), OSF/1 1.0.4
was being "serverized" in Grenoble, which in turn was turned into
a distributed system by my group.

OSF/1 AD is an offering, like all of OSF's technology.  This means
it is not necessarily a turn-key product.  It also had outside
contributors.  Intel and Locus Computer were key collaborators,
for obvious reasons.

And, there is lead time in each step of the pipeline.  AD has already
moved to version 1.0.3 (which is separate from the 1.0.4 OFS integrated
kernel version).  This is to be part of a future Intel release.  The next
major version of AD will be based upon the newest OSF/1 offering, (which
is still underway at present).  This will in turn be made available to
those using AD (Intel, et al).

From: jet@boxer.nas.nasa.gov (J. Eric Townsend)
> The source for libc.a that I have, which is included in our source for
> OSF/1 AD, appears to be optimized for the RS/6000 architecture.  It
> does not function correctly on the i860 architecture, and I could not
> get it to work correctly on the mips architecture.  It has been
> explained to me that this was fixed in OSF/1 quite a while back.

It would be interested to know what exactly you are talking about.
The RS/6000 is not a reference platform for OSF/1.  I have never seen
any "optimization" within the C library that favors a particular
architecture.

From: schoinas@fox.cs.wisc.edu (Yannis Schoinas)
> My biggest problem has to do with the mach calls that are supposed to 
> exist in the kernel. Currently, the OS is in a pre-release stage and
> if I am not mistaken, the first official release is not scheduled before 
> September. But I would like to know which mach calls, from the ones 
> described in the latest (MK12) OSF-CMU manuals, I should expect to find 
> at that time. That is do these manuals describe the calls of OSF/1AD to 
> any degree of accuracy?

Of course, the manuals to which you refer are those for the version of
the Mach 3 kernel in use.  In fact, the interfaces for the kernel used
by the AD project for AD release 1 (which, in turn, is what is being
used by Intel on the Paragon) have not changed and are not changing.
I still use the NMK12 manuals myself.  This "version" of Mach3 is
a branch off the CMU RCS tree, primarily with added bug fixes in the
NORMA and XMM code (in the OSF version) and missing the latest new
gizmos (from the CMU version).

There is a "new" Mach kernel spec being developed based around the
requirements of several DARPA-sponsored research groups.  This is
what things will be in the near future for the rest of the Mach 3
community.  However, AD won't be tied to these new interfaces until
the next major version.

As for Intel hiding the Mach interfaces from UNIX applications -
you'll have to ask them about their plans there.

BTW, the only external interfaces from OSF/1 AD are, in fact, the
AES (aka "API" aka man(2) and man(3)).  And in this, AD is 100%
source (and binary) compatible with OSF/1 1.0.4.  I.e., things
compiled on an 486 under the integrated kernel work on AD on the
same architecture.  And I'm pretty sure that things compiled on
a Hypercube work on the Paragon as well.

(disclaimer: all of the above is purely my view of the state of affairs
 and shouldn't be construed as anything more than such)

John
OSF Research Institute - Cambridge, MA



